EXT. HAL'S BACK GARDEN - DAY

A larch-lap fence, one panel newer than the rest. Beside it a rotary clothesline, empty, turning slightly.

HAL, sixties, on a stepladder with a spirit level held against the top rail like a doctor with a stethoscope.

TESSA, thirties, comes round the side of the house with an overnight bag and stops.

TESSA
Dad.

HAL
It's a quarter of an inch out over four foot.

TESSA
Dad, come down.

HAL
That's not weather. Weather doesn't do a quarter inch in a straight line.

TESSA
Nobody thinks it's weather.

HAL
He thinks I think it's weather.

INT. HAL'S KITCHEN - DAY

Tessa puts a brochure on the table. Sunlit lounge, a woman laughing at a jigsaw.

Hal reads the front of it and then reads the back of it and then squares it up with the edge of the table.

HAL
They've photographed that jigsaw from above. You can see it's only got the edge done.

TESSA
The boundary has to be clear before they'll list it. That's all. That's the only bit that's up to us.

HAL
It is clear.

TESSA
Then it's an afternoon.

HAL
It's been clear since nineteen ninety-four.

He gets up to fill a kettle that is already full.

EXT. HAL'S BACK GARDEN - DAY

DEL, forties, appears over the fence with the particular calm of a man who has taken advice.

DEL
Tessa. Heard you were up.

TESSA
Del.

DEL
Bit of a nightmare, all this.

TESSA
It's nine inches.

DEL
It's nine inches on the plan. It's a fence post through my mother's raspberries in real life, and I've got a dog who's found the gap, and one of these days he'll be on the road and then it won't be nine inches, will it, it'll be a dog.

HAL
(not turning round)
Dog's welcome.

DEL
He knows he is. That's half the problem.

INT. HAL'S FRONT ROOM - EVENING

Tessa on the floor with the deeds spread out, a torch, and a magnifier.

The conveyance plan. The red line. She measures with her thumb, twice.

She sits back.

The line goes where Del says it goes.

EXT. HAL'S BACK GARDEN - DAY

Tessa at the bottom of the garden with a trowel, digging under a hydrangea that has been there longer than she has.

The trowel hits iron.

She clears round it: a survey pin, driven flush, thirty years of soil on it, nine inches inside her father's fence.

And leaning against it, wrapped in a freezer bag, a jam jar. Inside the jar, a wedding ring.

Tessa sits down in the dirt.

She looks up the length of the garden. The pin. The old fence line. And on the wrong side of it now, exactly where it has always stood, her mother's clothesline.

INT. HAL'S KITCHEN - DAY

Tessa puts the pin on the table. Hal looks at it the way you look at a photograph of yourself you did not know had been taken.

TESSA
You moved it.

HAL
I put a fence up.

TESSA
Nine inches out. In ninety-four.

HAL
She wanted the line where the sun was. It was where the sun was.

TESSA
Dad.

HAL
Four hours of it, that corner. Everywhere else in this garden you'd get two.

TESSA
And you never told her.

HAL
She never asked. That's a different thing.

Tessa turns the jam jar over in her hand and does not ask about it, and he does not offer.

TESSA
I'm going to tell him.

HAL
You'll be telling him it's his.

TESSA
Yes.

EXT. DEL'S DRIVE - DAY

Tessa on the drive with the pin in her hand. Del in the doorway, a dog leaning on his leg.

TESSA
It's nine inches and it's yours and my dad put it there on purpose. There's a pin under the hydrangea if your surveyor wants it, and he won't have to look for it, I've left it standing up.

DEL
Right.

TESSA
The sale'll take longer now.

DEL
Probably.

TESSA
I'd still rather you heard it from us.

Del looks past her at the fence.

DEL
Why'd he do it?

TESSA
Sunlight.

EXT. HAL'S BACK GARDEN - NIGHT

Security light. Hal in a coat over pyjamas, taking the fence down panel by panel with a claw hammer, badly, one-handed, sixty-something.

Tessa comes out. She doesn't stop him.

She holds the next panel while he gets the nails out.

They work like that for a while, not talking, breath showing.

EXT. HAL'S BACK GARDEN - MORNING

No fence. Just two gardens, a raspberry row, and nine inches of nobody's grass.

The rotary line stands where it has stood since 1994, which is now, indisputably, Del's side.

Del's washing is on it. Shirts, a duvet cover, three pairs of small socks.

Hal in the kitchen window with a cup of tea, watching a duvet cover fill up like a sail in four hours of morning sun.

EXT. HAL'S BACK STEP - MORNING

Hal and Tessa on the step. The brochure is in her bag and she leaves it there.

She takes out the jam jar and holds it out.

Hal doesn't take it. He unscrews it, tips the ring into his palm, and then walks down the garden in his slippers.

He pegs it to the line, one wooden peg, at the corner.

Then he comes back and sits down and drinks his tea, and across nine inches of nobody's grass the ring goes round and round in the sun.
